Chamath Palihapitiya just raised a pretty serious concern about the AI boom and I think this could become one of the biggest issues facing the bond market over the next few years.

For context, the largest AI companies have accumulated an estimated $340 billion to $400 billion in debt, and the amount of money they need to raise is only getting bigger. Companies like Google, Amazon, and Meta are spending hundreds of billions building data centers, purchasing GPUs, expanding power infrastructure, and developing AI models. The problem is that even some of the most profitable companies in the world are reaching a point where their operating cash flow isn't enough to comfortably finance all that spending.

@chamath estimates that the additional financing these companies will need through the end of 2027 could equal roughly 30% of everything the US Treasury is expected to issue over that same period. Think about what that means. Some of the largest corporations in America are now competing with the U.S. government for capital from the same institutional investors, pension funds, insurance companies, and bond funds. If these companies continue flooding the bond market with hundreds of billions in new debt, investors may demand higher yields to absorb all that supply. That creates the potential for upward pressure on borrowing costs across the economy, including mortgages, corporate loans, and government debt. And higher yields would also put pressure on stock valuations, especially

